  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nProposing an entropy-based optimization method due to the combinatorial complexity and multi-dimensional optimization required to minimize peak convolution. Traditional LP methods showed limited progress (1.516), so this approach uses information entropy to systematically explore sequences that reduce peak auto-convolution. Similar to compressing a file, we seek sequences that evolve to minimize the required function. The key idea is to use entropy maximization under bounds on convolution magnitudes, which correlates with reduced peak convolution. This method touches the theoretical maximum perimeter length sequences can have while still having a bounded effect on the objective.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe treat sequences as distributions over finite support (coefficients) with fixed length n. Define P_\u03c6 for \u03c6 > 0, but we work directly with unconstrained real vectors. The optimization begins with uniform profile initialization, then adjusts coefficients proportionally to follow the gradient of the entropy function. We incorporate constraints based on observed peak values from the Fourier transform, related to peak auto-convolution bounds, through the use matrix. The primary step involves maximizing Tsallis entropy under exponential bounds derived from the convolution response.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize sequences of length n with uniform distribution (or prior initialization). Evaluate using evaluator.get_score(sequence). Compute gradient-free updates by: a) adjusting all coefficients proportionally based on the current best score, b) using reweighting and resampling to explore other distributions. Maintain candidate storage and update via entropy maximization (which directly minimizes peak convolution) until reaching timeout. Return best candidate found, enforced by re-running evaluator.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault initialization: uniform distribution for n=500 to 2000. Apply regularization through normalization processes. The main search orientation is balanced between sequence expansion and entropy optimization. Various convolutions and logarithmic transforms are applied judiciously only if windowed fits are not near theoretical bounds. Diversify by length and shape, with explicit handling for memory constraints and avoiding normalizations that might inadvertently bias the search towards higher scores.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach sequence is processed with a time budget specific to its length. Time is allocated as follows: longer sequences (n=2000) receive more time (up to 20% of total) but with resource limits. We fine-tune the scaling via timeouts and monitor for stagnation. For safety against arithmetic issues, we use clipping, vector translation, and Liapunov-like regularization. Direct fallback after n=500 fails is a constrained sequence.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEach sequence is explicitly re-validated through the evaluator. We re-run the score evaluation only for the candidate we wish to return. This candidate must be the one with the minimum score observed during trials. Print statements track progress and Select expressions that require fallbacks if needed.",
